WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.046 --> 00:00:08.796
(ballroom music)

00:00:10.618 --> 00:00:12.360
(DJ yells)

00:00:12.360 --> 00:00:14.900
- [Male Narrator] This is ballroom.

00:00:14.900 --> 00:00:17.400
No, not the classical
dances like waltz and tango.

00:00:19.040 --> 00:00:20.190
This is an underground scene

00:00:20.190 --> 00:00:21.810
that's been a safe space of expression

00:00:21.810 --> 00:00:23.300
for gay and trans people of color

00:00:23.300 --> 00:00:24.919
for decades.

00:00:24.919 --> 00:00:26.460
(DJ yells)

00:00:26.460 --> 00:00:27.360
It's kinda like a mix between

00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:29.960
a dance floor and a cat walk.

00:00:31.078 --> 00:00:34.078
(strong bass music)

00:00:38.787 --> 00:00:41.160
And this scene has created a whole

00:00:41.160 --> 00:00:42.880
new form of music.

00:00:42.880 --> 00:00:46.770
- Yeah, I'm usually like super

00:00:46.770 --> 00:00:48.193
afraid of cameras.

00:00:49.960 --> 00:00:50.857
- [Female Narrator] MikeQ is a touring DJ

00:00:50.857 --> 00:00:52.750
who has been producing ballroom tracks for

00:00:52.750 --> 00:00:54.530
more than a decade and is the founder

00:00:54.530 --> 00:00:56.400
of the record label Qween Beat.

00:00:57.543 --> 00:01:01.053
- I kinda feel like I'm talking to Diddy.

00:01:02.116 --> 00:01:02.950
(MikeQ laughs)

00:01:02.950 --> 00:01:07.763
Like, created the first record
label in ballroom music.

00:01:13.550 --> 00:01:15.380
So we're here in New
York to meet the dancers

00:01:15.380 --> 00:01:17.090
and musicians of the ballroom scene.

00:01:17.090 --> 00:01:18.680
And this week is special because it marks

00:01:18.680 --> 00:01:21.470
the 50th anniversary
for the stonewall riots.

00:01:21.470 --> 00:01:23.800
- [Female Narrator] Which
was a 1969 uprising of

00:01:23.800 --> 00:01:25.660
gay and trans people
that that took the fight

00:01:25.660 --> 00:01:28.140
for LGBTQ rights to the mainstream.

00:01:28.140 --> 00:01:29.450
- [Male Narrator] Since before stonewall,

00:01:29.450 --> 00:01:30.920
the ballroom scene has provided a place

00:01:30.920 --> 00:01:32.450
for the most marginalized people

00:01:32.450 --> 00:01:34.110
in American society could feel

00:01:34.110 --> 00:01:35.840
a sense of community and explore

00:01:35.840 --> 00:01:37.823
performances of gender and sexuality.

00:01:38.910 --> 00:01:40.530
Black and Latino, gay and trans people

00:01:40.530 --> 00:01:44.150
gather to walk or compete at balls.

00:01:44.150 --> 00:01:46.030
Contestants strut down makeshift runways

00:01:46.030 --> 00:01:48.100
wearing glamorous fashions.

00:01:48.100 --> 00:01:50.890
Voguing with its angular model inspired

00:01:50.890 --> 00:01:52.580
dance moves became a part of these runway

00:01:52.580 --> 00:01:54.130
walks in the 80's.

00:01:54.130 --> 00:01:56.040
Participants vie for titles like

00:01:56.040 --> 00:01:58.500
most real, best schoolgirl look,

00:01:58.500 --> 00:01:59.890
best military look.

00:01:59.890 --> 00:02:01.340
And sometimes they are part of a house

00:02:01.340 --> 00:02:03.520
or a family, like House of Ninja,

00:02:03.520 --> 00:02:07.050
House of LaBeija, or House of Xtravaganza.

00:02:07.050 --> 00:02:08.410
- [Female Narrator] For
many, a house provides

00:02:08.410 --> 00:02:10.230
a surrogate family when their real one

00:02:10.230 --> 00:02:11.370
has disowned them.

00:02:11.370 --> 00:02:13.530
Each house has a mother and father

00:02:13.530 --> 00:02:15.620
who provide mentorship for their children.

00:02:15.620 --> 00:02:18.610
- To be part of a house,
to be part of Xtravaganza,

00:02:18.610 --> 00:02:21.090
was family and looking out for each other.

00:02:21.090 --> 00:02:22.430
Looking out for our own.

00:02:22.430 --> 00:02:24.100
Defending our own.

00:02:24.100 --> 00:02:26.590
I am honored to even be here today to call

00:02:26.590 --> 00:02:30.250
myself a father of such
a beautiful organization.

00:02:30.250 --> 00:02:31.910
Jose Xtravaganza is the father

00:02:31.910 --> 00:02:33.630
of the House of Xtravaganza.

00:02:33.630 --> 00:02:35.090
He's been a part of the ballroom scene

00:02:35.090 --> 00:02:36.207
since the 1980's.

00:02:37.259 --> 00:02:40.160
- I was 16 years old
when first heard about

00:02:40.160 --> 00:02:42.690
the ballroom scene and waltzed down here,

00:02:42.690 --> 00:02:44.190
as a matter of fact.

00:02:44.190 --> 00:02:48.720
And so a whole, like, utopia of world.

00:02:48.720 --> 00:02:51.560
But see, as fathers when
the voguing comes to play,

00:02:51.560 --> 00:02:53.720
I go back to where it's from.

00:02:53.720 --> 00:02:56.850
Voguing is a feeling, it's an attitude.

00:02:56.850 --> 00:02:59.380
It's self expression as individuals.

00:02:59.380 --> 00:03:00.213
- [Female Narrator] Vogue is just one

00:03:00.213 --> 00:03:01.560
part of walking, but it might be

00:03:01.560 --> 00:03:03.170
the most well known.

00:03:03.170 --> 00:03:04.720
Named after the fashion magazine,

00:03:04.720 --> 00:03:06.460
voguing was introduced to the mainstream

00:03:06.460 --> 00:03:09.650
in 1990 when Madonna
released her track "Vogue".

00:03:09.650 --> 00:03:11.650
It was the dance style
that inspired the song,

00:03:11.650 --> 00:03:13.060
not the other way around.

00:03:13.060 --> 00:03:15.440
And when Madonna needed
a vogue expert she turned

00:03:15.440 --> 00:03:17.910
to Jose Xtravaganza who choreographed

00:03:17.910 --> 00:03:20.781
and appeared in the
music video for the song.

00:03:20.781 --> 00:03:22.010
- [Jose Xtravaganza] It was
a dance that was created

00:03:22.010 --> 00:03:25.470
on hope and dreams for
the community, you know?

00:03:25.470 --> 00:03:28.490
It was a dance that, you know,
no technique was necessary.

00:03:28.490 --> 00:03:29.960
You didn't have to study it at a school

00:03:29.960 --> 00:03:32.570
like other art forms of dance, you know?

00:03:32.570 --> 00:03:33.460
- [Female Narrator] At first, voguing

00:03:33.460 --> 00:03:35.050
was all about locking the body into

00:03:35.050 --> 00:03:36.850
perfectly straight lines and awkward

00:03:36.850 --> 00:03:38.790
but visually stunning positions.

00:03:38.790 --> 00:03:41.300
Kind of like the models in Vogue magazine.

00:03:41.300 --> 00:03:44.350
Back then, this was done
to disco or house music.

00:03:44.350 --> 00:03:47.200
So as a classical musician and just a

00:03:47.200 --> 00:03:49.810
music appreciator in general,

00:03:49.810 --> 00:03:52.250
I can, after hearing some ballroom tracks,

00:03:52.250 --> 00:03:55.160
I can recognize the similarity.

00:03:55.160 --> 00:03:57.330
The kinda vibe, the culture behind it.

00:03:57.330 --> 00:04:01.078
But I can't quite put my
finger on what exactly

00:04:01.078 --> 00:04:04.270
are the musical ingredients that go into

00:04:04.270 --> 00:04:06.048
creating a ballroom track.

00:04:06.048 --> 00:04:06.881
- [Male Narrator] A ballroom track.

00:04:06.881 --> 00:04:08.000
- Could you break it down
and in your own words,

00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:09.860
what is essential?

00:04:09.860 --> 00:04:12.720
- The music started out
with just house music

00:04:12.720 --> 00:04:14.920
that was adapted into ballroom

00:04:14.920 --> 00:04:17.390
before we got our own music.

00:04:17.390 --> 00:04:18.570
- [Female Narrator] The
most famous vogue track

00:04:18.570 --> 00:04:22.120
of the 1980s was "Love
Is The Message" by MFSB.

00:04:22.120 --> 00:04:24.210
But in the 1990s, "The Ha Dance" by

00:04:24.210 --> 00:04:26.500
Masters at Work emerged
as a quintessential

00:04:26.500 --> 00:04:29.690
vogue track and it's had
the ultimate sting power.

00:04:29.690 --> 00:04:31.370
- That track itself is just important.

00:04:31.370 --> 00:04:34.510
Like I said, it's just
music that was regular

00:04:34.510 --> 00:04:36.860
house music that was
brought into ballroom.

00:04:36.860 --> 00:04:38.297
- [Female Narrator]
MikeQ has said that 90%

00:04:38.297 --> 00:04:41.540
of the tracks he's produced
have been Ha-ly mixes.

00:04:41.540 --> 00:04:43.903
It's still considered the voguing song.

00:04:45.760 --> 00:04:49.660
- This came out in what 1991?

00:04:49.660 --> 00:04:51.350
- The Ha dance includes a few things that

00:04:51.350 --> 00:04:53.810
make it so iconic, vogue worthy,

00:04:53.810 --> 00:04:55.920
and representative of the genre.

00:04:55.920 --> 00:04:57.770
Minimal lyrics that allow the commentator

00:04:57.770 --> 00:04:59.670
to preside over all ball's proceedings.

00:04:59.670 --> 00:05:02.280
Calling out categories
and pumping up the crowd.

00:05:02.280 --> 00:05:05.060
(commentator yelling)

00:05:05.060 --> 00:05:06.850
A cymbal crash on every fourth beat

00:05:06.850 --> 00:05:09.050
that tells dancers when
to dip to the floor.

00:05:11.256 --> 00:05:12.869
And of course, the samples.

00:05:12.869 --> 00:05:15.282
- They were able to
catch that fourth beat,

00:05:15.282 --> 00:05:17.470
that sound, which we call the Ha Crash.

00:05:17.470 --> 00:05:18.303
- Okay.

00:05:19.386 --> 00:05:20.377
- Can you explain that further?

00:05:21.613 --> 00:05:22.580
(Ha music playing)

00:05:22.580 --> 00:05:23.413
- So, you hear it?

00:05:23.413 --> 00:05:26.313
One, two, three, four,
one, two, three, four.

00:05:30.220 --> 00:05:31.430
- [Male Narrator] That
sample you hear throughout

00:05:31.430 --> 00:05:34.940
the Ha Dance that iconic Ha
comes from "Trading Places".

00:05:34.940 --> 00:05:38.537
A 1983 movie starring Eddy
Murphy and Dan Aykroyd.

00:05:38.537 --> 00:05:42.300
(singing along to Ha music)

00:05:42.300 --> 00:05:44.850
The film doesn't hold up
under modern scrutiny.

00:05:44.850 --> 00:05:46.600
Aykroyd is in black face in this scene,

00:05:46.600 --> 00:05:48.860
but the song immortalized and transformed

00:05:48.860 --> 00:05:50.150
that moment into something for gay

00:05:50.150 --> 00:05:51.430
and trans people of color.

00:05:51.430 --> 00:05:52.430
- Though a lot of people think,

00:05:52.430 --> 00:05:54.280
oh you know sample the Ha, and there's

00:05:54.280 --> 00:05:56.400
just a Ha crash in there
and there's ballroom.

00:05:56.400 --> 00:05:58.500
But that's not it,
there's still that feeling

00:05:58.500 --> 00:06:01.460
that it has to embody
and I think you can only

00:06:01.460 --> 00:06:03.620
get that from visiting a ball

00:06:03.620 --> 00:06:05.840
and being, you know,
immersed in the culture

00:06:05.840 --> 00:06:09.068
and seeing how it is and feeling it.

00:06:09.068 --> 00:06:09.901
(car engine sound)

00:06:09.901 --> 00:06:10.760
- [Female Narrator]
MikeQ invited us to his

00:06:10.760 --> 00:06:12.760
monthly party House of
Vogue, so that we could

00:06:12.760 --> 00:06:15.150
experience the ballroom scene ourselves.

00:06:15.150 --> 00:06:17.400
We wanted to learn how
ballroom music has evolved

00:06:17.400 --> 00:06:20.439
from the early disco tracks
and when we got there,

00:06:20.439 --> 00:06:23.378
they were pretty excited to
have PBS in the building.

00:06:23.378 --> 00:06:24.211
(ballroom music playing)

00:06:24.211 --> 00:06:27.184
- PBS kids, got art?

00:06:27.184 --> 00:06:30.902
PBS kids, got art?

00:06:30.902 --> 00:06:34.719
Ladies and gentlemen, PBS
is in the mother building.

00:06:34.719 --> 00:06:37.128
(audience cheers)

00:06:37.128 --> 00:06:38.260
Got art?

00:06:38.260 --> 00:06:40.487
PBS kids, got art?

00:06:40.487 --> 00:06:43.017
I'm being interviewed by
the legendary iconic PBS.

00:06:43.017 --> 00:06:44.711
(laughing)

00:06:44.711 --> 00:06:47.628
(bass heavy music)

00:06:49.903 --> 00:06:52.765
- If you'd to pinpoint the hallmark

00:06:52.765 --> 00:06:55.759
sounds of ballroom music,
what would that be?

00:06:55.759 --> 00:06:58.201
- The hallmark sounds in ballroom music.

00:06:58.201 --> 00:07:00.660
I would have to say the
number one is the crash.

00:07:00.660 --> 00:07:02.800
- It's not like a regular drum crash.

00:07:02.800 --> 00:07:05.406
They like warp them, amplify them.

00:07:05.406 --> 00:07:06.810
It's like (makes crashing noise).

00:07:06.810 --> 00:07:08.509
You're like, whoa!

00:07:08.509 --> 00:07:09.342
- It's extra.

00:07:09.342 --> 00:07:10.253
- It sound like the matrix.

00:07:13.560 --> 00:07:14.839
- Wait, wait, wait, wait.

00:07:14.839 --> 00:07:16.501
(loud bass beating)

00:07:16.501 --> 00:07:17.865
Are you the sample?

00:07:17.865 --> 00:07:19.040
This is cool!

00:07:19.040 --> 00:07:19.873
- And wait till the crash come in.

00:07:22.225 --> 00:07:25.558
(singing along to song)

00:07:29.240 --> 00:07:31.159
That's that Jersey club I'm all about.

00:07:31.159 --> 00:07:34.070
(ballroom music)

00:07:34.070 --> 00:07:35.290
- [Male Narrator] Today's
ballroom tracks are a

00:07:35.290 --> 00:07:39.830
mixture of disco, funk,
hip hop, house, R&amp;B,

00:07:39.830 --> 00:07:41.520
and electronic music.

00:07:41.520 --> 00:07:43.250
- [MikeQ] DJ Vjuan Allur, he came up

00:07:43.250 --> 00:07:46.660
and started making remixes,
like actual remixes.

00:07:46.660 --> 00:07:49.587
So he would take from all
those older house tracks

00:07:49.587 --> 00:07:52.280
and what not, and put into his remixes.

00:07:52.280 --> 00:07:54.440
So that's kinda where the formula started.

00:07:54.440 --> 00:07:56.300
- [Male Narrator] The repetitive
bass beats and the cymbal

00:07:56.300 --> 00:07:58.390
crashes tell dancers when to strike a pose

00:07:58.390 --> 00:07:59.901
or hit the floor.

00:07:59.901 --> 00:08:02.651
(ballroom music)

00:08:08.006 --> 00:08:09.509
- [Male Narrator]
There's something special

00:08:09.509 --> 00:08:11.170
about the collaboration
with you and MikeQ,

00:08:11.170 --> 00:08:12.280
can you talk about that?

00:08:12.280 --> 00:08:14.170
- He's a genius, like honestly

00:08:14.170 --> 00:08:15.480
this has not been in ballroom,

00:08:15.480 --> 00:08:17.919
like there's not too many DJs

00:08:17.919 --> 00:08:19.650
that can connect with a performer.

00:08:19.650 --> 00:08:21.440
Like, you can put anything hot out there.

00:08:21.440 --> 00:08:23.830
You can vogue to it, but if the beat don't

00:08:23.830 --> 00:08:25.140
connect to what you're giving, you're not

00:08:25.140 --> 00:08:26.603
gonna get that cool story.

00:08:31.260 --> 00:08:32.800
- [Male Narrator] I was gonna ask you

00:08:32.800 --> 00:08:36.460
if you could like show us
where you get your sounds from.

00:08:36.460 --> 00:08:39.430
Your favorite 808's, this and then other.

00:08:39.430 --> 00:08:41.930
I only ask this so I can
steal your techniques so.

00:08:44.100 --> 00:08:46.920
(repetitive sounds)

00:08:46.920 --> 00:08:50.540
- So what I do first is,
because I have a long

00:08:50.540 --> 00:08:52.990
process the way that I do stuff.

00:08:52.990 --> 00:08:56.220
So I will create loops at first,

00:08:56.220 --> 00:08:58.733
and fruity loops here.

00:09:02.904 --> 00:09:04.650
- [Male Narrator] Okay, so you plug in.

00:09:04.650 --> 00:09:05.483
Okay, okay.

00:09:05.483 --> 00:09:08.911
- [MikeQ] Yeah, I get my
samples and I plug them in.

00:09:08.911 --> 00:09:10.086
Just this way.

00:09:10.086 --> 00:09:11.610
- [Male Narrator] So
they're in increments,

00:09:11.610 --> 00:09:13.720
it's like 16th notes.

00:09:13.720 --> 00:09:15.970
One and a two and a three, across the top.

00:09:15.970 --> 00:09:17.909
You know what I'm saying,
like in groups of four?

00:09:17.909 --> 00:09:18.790
One and a two and a three and a four.

00:09:18.790 --> 00:09:20.629
So each of these instruments

00:09:20.629 --> 00:09:22.070
is triggered at different 16th.

00:09:22.070 --> 00:09:22.980
- Oh.

00:09:22.980 --> 00:09:24.300
- You see what I'm saying?

00:09:24.300 --> 00:09:26.850
- I've never seen something like this.

00:09:26.850 --> 00:09:28.930
- After that, just start adding.

00:09:28.930 --> 00:09:30.620
You know, I'll just add little elements

00:09:30.620 --> 00:09:32.960
whatever I hear, like in my head,

00:09:32.960 --> 00:09:37.252
I'll picture people voguing a lot.

00:09:37.252 --> 00:09:38.371
- [Male Narrator] Work, yeah, yeah.

00:09:38.371 --> 00:09:40.360
- Or just like through the art of voguing

00:09:40.360 --> 00:09:42.590
or being at a ball, that's
what I'm thinking about

00:09:42.590 --> 00:09:44.677
when I'm making tracks.

00:09:44.677 --> 00:09:47.427
(ballroom music)

00:09:50.967 --> 00:09:53.610
- One word that we kinda
agreed on in the car

00:09:53.610 --> 00:09:58.610
is ballroom music has
an unmatched attitude.

00:09:58.731 --> 00:10:01.432
If there was a big word,
I would just say attitude.

00:10:01.432 --> 00:10:02.854
- [MikeQ] Okay, I like that.

00:10:02.854 --> 00:10:03.687
- [Male Narrator] I would say attitude.

00:10:03.687 --> 00:10:05.564
- [Female Narrator] Attitude, very catchy,

00:10:05.564 --> 00:10:08.780
very fun, and relaxed but intense.

00:10:08.780 --> 00:10:11.320
- Ballroom tracks are meant
to inspire competition.

00:10:11.320 --> 00:10:13.540
They're meant to make you feel
fierce when you hear them.

00:10:13.540 --> 00:10:15.610
Like you're about to do a
battle on the dance floor.

00:10:15.610 --> 00:10:17.900
That's because voguing
started as a way to work out

00:10:17.900 --> 00:10:19.770
differences between rivals.

00:10:19.770 --> 00:10:22.330
In "Paris Is Burning"
Willi Ninja, known as the

00:10:22.330 --> 00:10:24.740
godfather of voguing,
says that when two or more

00:10:24.740 --> 00:10:26.530
people are voguing on the dance floor,

00:10:26.530 --> 00:10:29.760
whoever had the best moves
was throwing the best shade.

00:10:29.760 --> 00:10:32.380
- Sometimes people throw
shade just by voguing,

00:10:32.380 --> 00:10:36.150
like say you're at LSS if I call out LA

00:10:37.353 --> 00:10:38.687
that LA come out and show who he is

00:10:38.687 --> 00:10:40.707
and you just pop up like, oh!

00:10:41.547 --> 00:10:43.322
(laughs)

00:10:43.322 --> 00:10:44.155
- Okay!

00:10:44.155 --> 00:10:44.988
- Like, bye!

00:10:44.988 --> 00:10:45.883
That's throwing shade.

00:10:45.883 --> 00:10:47.133
- That's shade!

00:10:49.630 --> 00:10:51.740
- [Female Narrator] And when
you throw shade is that always

00:10:51.740 --> 00:10:54.695
a negative thing or is
that kind of a sign?

00:10:54.695 --> 00:10:55.610
- [Ash B] You know what I
had to learn in the ballroom

00:10:55.610 --> 00:10:58.710
I had to grow tough skin
because I'm from the hood,

00:10:58.710 --> 00:11:00.790
so people throwing shade
to me kinda feel like,

00:11:00.790 --> 00:11:02.460
yo you coming at me?

00:11:02.460 --> 00:11:05.090
It's like, yo why you coming at me?

00:11:05.090 --> 00:11:08.830
But I realized it's
really like sportsmanship.

00:11:08.830 --> 00:11:11.440
It's like playing basketball
and somebody smack you

00:11:11.440 --> 00:11:13.740
on the butt, like it
comes with the territory.

00:11:15.040 --> 00:11:17.720
- The tracks are also meant
to encourage and celebrate

00:11:17.720 --> 00:11:18.853
trans femininity.

00:11:19.689 --> 00:11:21.690
Some of the most classic
ballroom tracks include samples

00:11:21.690 --> 00:11:23.340
that have phrases and words in them

00:11:23.340 --> 00:11:25.710
